Object.assign(ENEMIES,{
 frayed:{name:"The Frayed One",hp:26,
  open:"She comes up out of the reed-water all wrong angles and old silk, and her eyes find your hair, and she makes a sound that was once a word. \u201CGold,\u201D she says. \u201CGold gold gold. They'll WIND you.\u201D",
  moves:[{n:"Unraveling Touch",kind:"stance",st:"flow",text:"Her outline frays and runs \u2014 she is more current than woman now.",tele:"Her form is fraying into current \u2014 nothing will hold her."},
   {n:"Thread-drink",d:4,drain:2},
   {n:"Frayed lash",d:6},
   {n:"Reknitting",kind:"heal",h:6,text:"She pulls your stolen threads into her own wounds, knitting them shut with your gold.",tele:"She is gathering your stolen threads \u2014 she means to mend herself with them."}]}});


SC["a3c16_marsh"]={t:()=>SYS(`CHAPTER SIXTEEN — THE OTHER FOREIGNER`)+P(`The marshes between Clearwater and the lake country have a haunting, and the haunting has habits.`)+P(`Fishing nets unravel overnight — not torn: <i>untied</i>, every knot opened in order. Mooring lines slip. A bandit crew that wintered in the reeds came out in spring two men short and won't say anything except that the marsh hums. The marsh-folk leave rice on flat stones and call her the Reed Wife, and the rice is always gone by morning, and so is anything in the offering with thread in it — ribbon, cord, the silk tassel off a prayer charm. Never the food first. The thread first.`)+P(`You pole into the reed-maze alone, because whatever untied those nets in order can hear what you can hear, and crowds make it scream.`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Follow the hum.`,go:"a3c16_meet"}]};

SC["a3c16_meet"]={img:"marsh",
 t:()=>P(`You find her at the drowned shrine at the marsh's heart, and the seeing is like looking into a cracked mirror.`)+P(`She is — was — like you. A foreign soul; the Weave bends around her the way it bends around no native-born thing. And she is <i>torn</i>: her soul-thread ends in a frayed horsetail of filaments where it was once wound onto a loom and ripped loose by main force. Half of her is still trying to arrive somewhere. The fray never closes. Through it, you understand in one cold drop, she hears what you hear — except ALL of it, ALWAYS, every thread in the marsh and the river and the sky screaming its tension into the open wound of her, forty years of a noise that you have only ever heard as music.`)+P(`Clearwater's frame holds nine at a time; its ledger ran past forty. She was the forty-first — the one that got away, at the only price the getting-away ever costs.`)+P(`She circles you in the reed-water, hungry and terrified, saying the one word she has left. The next minute goes one of two ways, and you only get to pick if you're fast and deep enough to weave a silence she can stand inside.`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Weave the silence — a dome of stilled threads, the first quiet she's known in forty years — and stand in it with her, hands open.`,req:()=>(G.insight>=10&&G.heart>=6),rq:"(needs Insight 10 and Heart 6)",go:"a3c16_calmed"},
  {id:"c1",l:`Hold your ground. If she comes, she comes — survive her first.`,go:"a3c16_fight"}]};

SC["a3c16_fight"]={combat:"frayed",
 win:"a3c16_won",
 lose:"a3c16_lost",
 t:()=>""};

SC["a3c16_won"]={t:()=>P(`She goes down in the shallow water, frayed and gasping, too spent to flee, and your last knot hangs ready, and this is the choice the marsh has been saving for you.`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Weave the silence now, while she's too weak to bolt — quiet, the first in forty years, as a victor's gift.`,req:()=>(G.insight>=10),rq:"(needs Insight 10)",go:"a3c16_calmed"},
  {id:"c1",l:`End it. Cleanly, completely — the mercy the Loom never gave her.`,do:()=>{G.f.a3_feral="ended";addIns(1);note("Her thread, released, doesn't keen like the furnace souls did. It sighs. The fray closes at last on nothing, and the marsh goes quiet for the first night in forty years, and you sit at the drowned shrine until dawn learning exactly how much of yourself you spent on this — and that you'd spend it again, and what that means about you now.");},go:"a3c16_after"},
  {id:"c2",l:`Bind her and back away. Not your knot to tie or cut.`,do:()=>{G.f.a3_feral="fled";note("The binding holds an hour past your leaving — long enough. By the next moon the Reed Wife stories start again, two marshes further from the river road. You didn't save her. You didn't end her. Some audits don't balance; you carry the remainder.");},go:"a3c16_after"}]};

SC["a3c16_lost"]={t:()=>P(`The thread-drink takes more than threads. You wake in your beached boat at dawn, drained hollow, every pattern aching like a pulled tooth — alive, because she chose it.`)+P(`She is crouched on the bow, gone the moment you focus, but her last sound stays: she looked at your gold thread, whole and bright and KEPT, and she made a noise that was not hunger. It was grief. And then, distinctly, in an accent forty years drowned: <i>“Run before they wind you, kid.”</i>`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`She knew what you are. She remembered her own arriving.`,do:()=>{G.f.a3_feral="fled";G.hp=Math.min(G.maxhp,G.hp+6);note("+6 HP, slowly. The marsh-folk find you, feed you, and ask no questions — the Reed Wife's survivors never have answers anyway.");},go:"a3c16_after"}]};

SC["a3c16_calmed"]={t:()=>P(`The silence lands on her like the first rain after a drought year.`)+P(`She stands in it swaying, eyes shut, mouth open, forty years of clenching letting go muscle by muscle — and then she sits down in the reed-water and weeps the way children weep, enormously, without management, and you stand holding the dome of stilled threads steady and let her.`)+P(`Her name does not come back; the loom took the part that kept names. What comes back, in pieces, over a night of shared rice and held silence: she fell out of a different sky — her words for home are wrong for yours, trains but no porch lights — washed up forty years ago, was taken in by a kind river sect, fed warm meals at the gate for a month. <i>Fattened.</i> Wound. And torn free by her own fraying hands when the winding was half done, because half of her — she taps the horsetail fray with terrible matter-of-factness — “is still in the room with the white silk lady.”`)+P(`You go very still. “The inspector?”`)+P(`“Comes in autumn,” she agrees, in the singsong of a schedule learned from inside it. “Counts the brackets. Smells of mulberry. Writes with a needle, writes ON the loom. The Court likes the rare ones alive, gold-thread.” Her eyes open, and for one moment they are perfectly, terribly sane. “They'll invite you first. They invited me. Politeness is the net.”`)+SYS(`The Frayed One is calmed — your first source on the Court, and the Court's case made flesh: foreign souls DO fray the Weave. She felt herself doing it, all forty years.`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Stay until dawn. Learn everything she can bear to say.`,do:()=>{G.f.a3_feral="calmed";addIns(1);},go:"a3c16_after"}]};

SC["a3c16_after"]={t:()=>P(`Poling out of the marsh at sunrise, you finally let yourself think the thought you've been holding underwater for a day:`)+P(`That's you. Subtract the basement. Subtract Bao's bet on a stranger, Mei's congee, Tang's white pot, an old woman's humming, one ferryman's kindness on one grey morning — subtract every knot anyone ever tied to you, and that's where the road goes: a hum in a marsh, untying other people's nets in order, hoarding thread against a cold that never leaves. The Loom drops people it doesn't catch. You were caught. You have spent two years thinking you saved yourself, and the marsh just spent a day correcting the record.`)+P(`The lake country opens ahead, pagodas and water-roads and ten thousand boats, and somewhere in it: a riverhouse address, a buyer of golden stories, a white silk lady who writes with a needle.`)+((G.f.a3_warned)?(P(`And somewhere ahead of you on this same water: one frightened abbot, rowing toward her with your description in his mouth.`)):("")),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`On to the lake country.`,go:"a3c17_lamp"}]};